From the sudoingX recipe (github.com/sudoingX/dgx-spark-deepseek-v41, cc59c36, patches/experimental/), unchanged below.
Applied in this image but off (VLLM_DSV41_TRITON_VERIFY unset); not measured on this setup.

Ours (dsv41 recipe), Apache-2.0, against vLLM main 6e517b15 + patches 0001-0021.
VLLM_DSV41_TRITON_VERIFY=1 (default off): on SM12x (GB10), DeepseekV4FlashInferSM120Attention sends its decode-form calls
(where the target's DSpark verify rows land; the drafter's too) and, in _forward_prefill, the chunks made only of short
queries to the EXL3 plugin's Triton sparse attention. (The first version hooked _forward_prefill alone and never ran:
LOG Oct 11 14:45.) Prefill-path rule:
prefill chunks made only of short queries (DSpark verify rows, up to _FLAT_MAX_Q = 8 tokens a request; V4.1 files
them under prefill) to the EXL3 plugin's Triton sparse attention (vllm_exl3_moe/sparse_attn.py) instead of
flashinfer_trtllm_batch_decode_sparse_mla_dsv4. FlashInfer's SM120 planner routes these 6-row calls by its calibrated
decode/prefill crossover to prefill_mg_dual, ~49 us a layer in 38 layers; forcing its decode kernel measured slower
(lever fidec, LOG Oct 11 13:32). The Triton kernel reads the same fp8_ds_mla pages (584 B records), per-token SWA and
compressed indices and lengths, the sink and the scale, and writes the same [T, H, 512] output (rope part rotated).
Rows past a request's DSpark depth (vLLM's is_padding, patch 0010) skip their attention (zeros; live rows keep their
bits, and causal attention means no live row reads a dead one). Rounding class (another accumulation order): judged on
the oracle's noise bars. The short-query test reads the host-side query_start_loc, so it is fixed at capture.
Test: patches/exl3_moe/tests/test_sparse_attn_gpu.py (reference); scripts/bench/sparse_attn_vs_flashinfer.py (GB10).

